\newlabel{fig:cache}{{9.1a}{103}{Depiction of the ordered dictionary used as the cache data structure}{figure.caption.116}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:cache}{{a}{103}{Depiction of the ordered dictionary used as the cache data structure}{figure.caption.116}{}}
\newlabel{fig:cache_hit}{{9.1b}{103}{Cache state after a cache hit moves the requested element to the front}{figure.caption.116}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:cache_hit}{{b}{103}{Cache state after a cache hit moves the requested element to the front}{figure.caption.116}{}}
\newlabel{fig:cache_miss}{{9.1c}{103}{Cache state after a cache miss removes the rear element and inserts the new element at the front}{figure.caption.116}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:cache_miss}{{c}{103}{Cache state after a cache miss removes the rear element and inserts the new element at the front}{figure.caption.116}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces The LRU caching policy implemented via an ordered dictionary. A key in the dictionary is formed by a canonical representation of two molecular graphs $(m_1,m_2)$ and the index of the reaction template $r$, while each value is a list containing the right hand sides of the reaction instances computed by applying all matches found between the left hand side of $r$ and the union of the molecular graphs $m_1\cup m_2$. For brevity, the keys are represented by single digit numbers, and the values by abstracted lists. Note that a value might be null for some keys, if the left hand side of $r$ does not match $m_1\cup m_2$. A cache hit results in reordering the entries in the dictionary, by moving the matched query to the front of the dictionary as shown in Figure~\ref {fig:cache_hit}. On a cache miss, the least recently used element (which is the element at the rear of the dictionary) is removed and the new element is added to the front of the dictionary as shown in Figure~\ref {fig:cache_miss}.}}{103}{figure.caption.116}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cache_model}{{9.1}{103}{The LRU caching policy implemented via an ordered dictionary. A key in the dictionary is formed by a canonical representation of two molecular graphs $(m_1,m_2)$ and the index of the reaction template $r$, while each value is a list containing the right hand sides of the reaction instances computed by applying all matches found between the left hand side of $r$ and the union of the molecular graphs $m_1\cup m_2$. For brevity, the keys are represented by single digit numbers, and the values by abstracted lists. Note that a value might be null for some keys, if the left hand side of $r$ does not match $m_1\cup m_2$. A cache hit results in reordering the entries in the dictionary, by moving the matched query to the front of the dictionary as shown in Figure~\ref {fig:cache_hit}. On a cache miss, the least recently used element (which is the element at the rear of the dictionary) is removed and the new element is added to the front of the dictionary as shown in Figure~\ref {fig:cache_miss}}{figure.caption.116}{}}
